window.docContents[26].resolve({"/part-1-foundations/ch02-03-ultrapower/#ch02-03-ultrapower":{"contents":"How do we actually build \\mathbb{R}^* without hand-waving? A natural first try is the ring of all real sequences \\mathbb{R}^{\\mathbb{N}} under termwise addition and multiplication: the sequence \\varepsilon = (1, \\tfrac{1}{2}, \\tfrac{1}{3}, \\dots) shrinks faster than every positive constant sequence (r, r, r, \\dots), while \\omega = (1, 2, 3, \\dots) outgrows every constant sequence.\n\nUnfortunately, raw \\mathbb{R}^{\\mathbb{N}} is not a field! The interlacing indicator sequences\n\ne = (1, 0, 1, 0, \\dots) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad o = (0, 1, 0, 1, \\dots)\n\nare both nonzero, yet their product is e \\cdot o = (0, 0, 0, \\dots). To quotient out zero divisors while making the order total, we must decide for every subset A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N} whether A or its complement A^c counts as “large.”\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lectures on the Hyperreals — Interactive Lean 4 & Spatial Edition\u0009Part I — Foundations of the Hyperreal Line","header":"1.2. Chapters 2 & 3: Large Sets, Ultrafilters & the Ultrapower  <missing>","id":"/part-1-foundations/ch02-03-ultrapower/#ch02-03-ultrapower"}});